  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1249 PM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nWednesday will be the warmest day, but Thursday will be close behind \nbefore we move into an active weekend. High pressure will amplify \nover the Panhandles on Wednesday and Thursday leading to highs in \nthe mid 70s to mid 80s with little to no rain chances.  As we \napproach Thursday evening a low pressure system coming in off the \nSouthern California coast will track across southern NV and bring \nanother swath of moisture that's projected to start moving into the \nsouthwest Panhandles as early as Thursday night, but the more likely \ntime of arrival will be Friday.  Given the strong south southwest \nflow with this, it will have a very gradual expansion westward, so \nmuch that there may not be much precipitation along and east of a \nGuymon to Amarillo line, where further west we could see continuous \nrain through the afternoon and evening.  Things get a little dicey \nas we head into Saturday and Sunday, but these are the better \nchances of precipitation across the entire Panhandles, and there \ncould be excessive rain involved, so localized flooding concerns are \nsomething to keep in mind.  But there is a chance for some or all of \nthe Panhandle to get a dry slot and miss out all together.  Based on \nthe pattern history we've been through, the dry slot scenario is \ncurrently less favored, but will not ever rule that out.  Right now \nit looks like a 70/30 split leaning towards a better chance of \ngetting rain this weekend. \n\nWeber",
